   "info": "Built for the 1984 Winter Olympics: 1,300 m of concrete trough down Trebević, thirteen curves, the Omega and the Double S among them. In the siege it was an artillery position. Abandoned since, it is the city's longest wall of graffiti."
  },
  {
   "name": "The Koševo stadium",
   "aka": [
    "Asim Ferhatović Hase stadium",
    "Olympic stadium"
   ],
   "at": [
    43.873779,
    18.408334
   ],
   "model": "stadium",
   "a": 120,
   "b": 103,
   "rows": 14,
   "axis": 20,
   "clear": [
    110,
    14
   ],
   "info": "Where the XIV Winter Olympics were opened on 8 February 1984, the figure skater Sanda Dubravčić lighting the cauldron. In the siege its training pitches became a cemetery."
  },
  {
   "name": "Zetra",
   "aka": [
    "Olimpijska dvorana Juan Antonio Samaranch"
   ],
   "at": [
    43.87182,
    18.40953
   ],
   "info": "The Olympic ice hall of 1984, where Torvill and Dean skated Bolero. Burned in 1992, its timber used as coffins; rebuilt in 1999."
  },
  {
   "name": "The White Bastion",
   "aka": [
    "Bijela tabija"
   ],
   "at": [
    43.85937,
    18.44455
   ],
   "info": "The ruined fortress over the eastern end of the old town, on the road to Istanbul, guarding the Miljacka's gorge."
  },
  {
   "name": "The Inat House",
   "aka": [
    "Inat kuća",
    "the House of Spite"
   ],
   "at": [
    43.85856,
    18.43425
   ],
   "info": "In the 1890s the city wanted its site for the City Hall. The owner agreed to go only if his house went with him, stone by stone, across the river: here it stands, and the name is spite."
  },
  {
   "name": "Morića Han",
   "at": [
    43.85967,
    18.43011
   ],
   "info": "The last of Baščaršija's caravanserais, from the sixteenth century: the courtyard, the stables, and the rooms for travellers upstairs."
  },
  {
   "name": "The Old Orthodox Church",
   "aka": [
    "Stara pravoslavna crkva"
   ],
   "at": [
    43.86026,
    18.43
   ],
   "info": "The church of the Holy Archangels Michael and Gabriel, sixteenth century: low and walled, as the Ottoman law asked of churches."
  },
  {
   "name": "The Orthodox Cathedral",
   "aka": [
    "Saborna crkva"
   ],
   "at": [
    43.85822,
    18.42519
   ],
   "info": "The Cathedral of the Nativity of the Theotokos, 1872, the largest Orthodox church in the city."
  },
  {
   "name": "Markale",
   "aka": [
    "Gradska tržnica Markale"
   ],
   "at": [
    43.85902,
    18.42335
   ],
   "info": "The city's market. Two shells struck it during the siege, in February 1994 and August 1995, killing 111 people; the second brought NATO's air strikes, and the end of the siege."
  },
  {
   "name": "The Ashkenazi synagogue",
   "aka": [
    "Aškenaska sinagoga"
   ],
   "at": [
    43.856322,
    18.425043
   ],
   "info": "1902, in the same Moorish style as the City Hall, on the river's south bank: the city's working synagogue. The Sarajevo Haggadah, carried here by Jews expelled from Spain, is in the National Museum."
  },
  {
   "name": "The Meeting of Cultures",
   "aka": [
    "Sarajevo - Meeting of Cultures"
   ],
   "at": [
    43.85935,
    18.42816
   ],
   "info": "A line across the paving where Ferhadija becomes Sarači. Look west and the street is Vienna's: four-storey blocks, shop windows, the cathedral. Look east and it is Istanbul's: low wooden shops, the bezistan, Gazi Husrev-beg's minaret. The city grew one way for four hundred years and the other for forty."
  },
  {
   "name": "A Sarajevo rose",
   "aka": [
    "Sarajevska ruža"
   ],
   "at": [
    43.85898,
    18.42345
   ],
   "info": "Where a mortar shell struck the pavement and killed someone in the siege, its scar was filled with red resin, a splash like a flower. There were a couple of hundred; the ones left are repainted every year. This one, by the market at Markale, is one of the best known."
  },
  {
   "name": "The Kovači martyrs' cemetery",
   "aka": [
    "Šehidsko mezarje Kovači"
   ],
   "at": [
    43.861364,
    18.435492
   ],
   "info": "The cemetery of the city's defenders on the hill under the Yellow Bastion: rows of white nišani, the dates on them nearly all 1992 to 1995. Alija Izetbegović, the wartime president, is buried here."
  },
  {
   "name": "Bare cemetery",
   "aka": [
    "Gradsko groblje Bare"
   ],
   "at": [
    43.878673,
    18.397858
   ],
   "info": "The city cemetery since 1966, of every faith: nišani, crosses and stars side by side up the hill. The siege filled it."
  },
  {
   "name": "The Old Jewish Cemetery",
   "aka": [
    "Jevrejsko groblje"
   ],
   "at": [
    43.850953,
    18.407004
   ],
   "info": "Founded in the 1630s on the slope of Trebević: over 3,800 flat stones, some carved with Hebrew and Ladino, the second largest Jewish cemetery in Europe. In the siege it was on the front line."
  }
 ],
